::MCR_U06_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Pretest_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
A side changes from 4 cm to 12 cm in a similar figure. What is the scale factor?
{
~A. 8#This subtracts side lengths. Scale factor is multiplicative.
=B. 3#Correct. The scale factor is 12 divided by 4.
~C. 16#This adds side lengths. Use a ratio.
~D. 48#This multiplies side lengths, which does not give the scale factor.
}
::MCR_U06_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Pretest_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the distance from (2, 5) to (9, 5)?
{
~A. 11 units#This adds x-values. Horizontal distance is the difference.
~B. 4 units#This compares y-values instead of x-values.
~C. 9 units#This uses one coordinate instead of the distance.
=D. 7 units#Correct. The y-values match, so 9 - 2 \= 7.
}
::MCR_U06_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Pretest_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4
Question:
A rectangle is 8 ft by 6 ft. What is its area?
{
=A. 48 square ft#Correct. Area \= 8 x 6 \= 48 square ft.
~B. 14 square ft#This adds dimensions. Area requires multiplication.
~C. 28 square ft#This is perimeter reasoning.
~D. 48 cubic ft#The number is correct, but area uses square units.
}
::MCR_U06_Pretest_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Pretest_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.5
Question:
Which situation asks for surface area?
{
~A. filling a tank#Filling asks for volume.
~B. measuring a board length#This asks for length.
=C. wrapping a box#Correct. Wrapping covers outside faces, so it asks for surface area.
~D. finding floor space#Floor space asks for area.
}
::MCR_U06_Pretest_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Pretest_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A prism is 3 ft by 4 ft by 5 ft. What is its volume?
{
=A. 60 cubic ft#Correct. Volume \= 3 x 4 x 5 \= 60 cubic ft.
~B. 12 cubic ft#This uses only two dimensions.
~C. 60 square ft#The number is correct, but volume uses cubic units.
~D. 15 cubic ft#This adds dimensions.
}
::MCR_U06_Pretest_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Pretest_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A right triangle has legs 5 and 12. What is the hypotenuse?
{
~A. 17#This adds the legs. Use the Pythagorean Theorem.
=B. 13#Correct. 5² + 12² \= 169, and sqrt(169) \= 13.
~C. 60#This multiplies the legs.
~D. 169#This is c², not c.
}
::MCR_U06_Pretest_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Pretest_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4; MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
Which unit should label volume?
{
~A. square inches#Square inches measure area.
~B. inches#Inches measure length.
~C. dollars#Dollars measure cost.
=D. cubic inches#Correct. Volume uses cubic units.
}
::MCR_U06_Pretest_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Pretest_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.1; MLA.MCR.GM.3
Question:
On a scale drawing, 1 inch represents 6 feet. What does 4 inches represent?
{
=A. 24 feet#Correct. 4 x 6 \= 24 feet.
~B. 10 feet#This adds 6 and 4.
~C. 6 feet#This represents only 1 inch.
~D. 2 feet#This divides instead of multiplying.
}
::MCR_U06_Pretest_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Pretest_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6
Question:
A design volume must be at most 80 cubic ft. The calculated volume is 75 cubic ft. What is true?
{
~A. It fails the constraint.#It does not fail because 75 <\= 80.
~B. It cannot be checked.#The units match, so it can be checked.
=C. It meets the constraint.#Correct. 75 is less than or equal to 80.
~D. It needs square feet.#The problem is about volume, so cubic feet are appropriate.
}
::MCR_U06_Pretest_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U06_Pretest_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which ratio compares opposite to hypotenuse?
{
~A. cosine#Cosine compares adjacent to hypotenuse.
=B. sine#Correct. Sine compares opposite to hypotenuse.
~C. tangent#Tangent compares opposite to adjacent.
~D. perimeter#Perimeter is not a trigonometric ratio.
}
